  "id": "COMPOUND:29364 - PROTEIN:3049",
  "verbalization": "Oxindole is implicated in the interaction with Cytochrome c4 through a series of molecular targets, primarily Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits. These subunits are identified as targets of multiple oxindole derivatives, including (4aS,6aR,6aS,6bR,8aR,10S,12aS,14bS)-2,2,6a,6b,9,9,12a-heptamethyl-10-octanoyloxy-13-oxo-3,4,5,6,6a,7,8,8a,10,11,12,14b-dodecahydro-1H-picene-4a-carboxylic acid, (2E,6E)-3,7,11-Trimethyl-9-(phenylsulfonyl)-2,6,10-dodecatrien-1-ol, and 6-O-Acetyldihydrohelenalin. This suggests that Oxindole may act as a broad-acting compound that modulates the activity of these ATPase subunits, which in turn may influence the function of Cytochrome c4. The shared target of these oxindole derivatives with Cytochrome c4 implies a potential mechanism by which Oxindole could exert its effects, possibly through the modulation of ion transport processes that are critical for cellular function. The evidence supports a direct interaction between Oxindole and Cytochrome c4 via its ability to target and influence the activity of Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits, highlighting a key pathway in the pharmacological action of Oxindole.",
